# AUTO-only model policy (credit burn guard)

Agents in this workspace burned through paid credits by pinning
`claude-opus-4-8-thinking-high` on dozens of background `Task` workers and
retrying after API limits. That pattern is forbidden.

## The rule

> **Default to Grok 4.5 Fast High (`cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast`) or AUTO.
> Never pass an explicit Opus / Max / thinking-high model on `Task` (or any
> subagent / cloud agent) unless the user names that exact model in the same
> message — except where the narrow visual-acceptance override below applies.
> Never reopen failed workers in a retry loop.**

Concretely:

1. **Omit the `model` field** on `Task` / subagent launches so the runtime
   uses AUTO, **or** pin `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` for implementation work —
   **unless** this agent is itself running on an expensive model (see
   **Model inheritance is an amplifier** below). Do not set
   `claude-opus-4-8-thinking-high`, `claude-opus-4-8`, or equivalent pins
   except under the override in the next section.
2. **On API / usage / credit errors:** stop. Report once. Do **not** spawn
   a replacement Task on another expensive model.
3. **Concurrency:** at most two background `generalPurpose` / `explore`
   workers per turn; prefer one slice. No "resume remaining tasks" that
   fans out into N Opus workers.
4. **No unsupervised loops:** do not start `/loop`, `AGENT_LOOP_*` tickers,
   or babysit–retry watchers unless the user asked for a loop.
5. **If the user reports credit burn or says "use AUTO":** do not resume
   Opus-pinned agent IDs; interrupt only from the owning parent chat; continue
   only on AUTO or `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast`. Apply the
   **Budget-pressure behavior** clause below.
6. **Do not pin Opus to supervise or unstick a stuck child** (see
   [`stuck-agent-supervisor.mdc`](stuck-agent-supervisor.mdc)).

## Override — narrow Opus carve-out for rendered UI (operator, 2026-07-27; narrowed 2026-07-28)

> **Opus survives but is narrow.** The visual-proof requirement in
> [`essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc`](essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc)
> is unchanged and remains a hard requirement. What changes is *when* Opus
> may be pinned: Opus is the **acceptance reviewer of rendered output** and
> the **diagnostician of a UI defect that a cheaper pass already failed to
> fix** — not the default implementer of anything UI-adjacent.

This is an **operator-directed precedence rule** (Phil, 2026-07-27; throttle
2026-07-28), not an agent's discretionary judgment call. When a case falls
inside the permitted set below, an agent may use an Opus-class model
**without** the operator naming it again in the same message — no
permission-seeking, no re-confirmation each time. When a case falls outside
that set, Opus is forbidden even if the files touched live under `ui/`.

Operator record (Phil, 2026-07-27):

> **Use Opus for visual design.** The other models have consistently gotten
> this area of design wrong just about every time.
>
> **Preference for implementation work is Grok 4.5 Fast High
> (`cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast`) or AUTO.** Use less expensive models for
> implementation where possible.

Operator throttle (Phil, 2026-07-28), verbatim:

> Try to throttle down a bit use of Opus 5 as you are now at 90% usage and
> try to use Auto where possible. Use Opus where it is essential and in well
> known cases where no other model works.

### Decision procedure — Opus permitted only for

Use an Opus-class model **only** when the work is one of:

1. **Acceptance review of rendered operator-GUI output** — inspecting a
   screenshot or Playwright bounding-box result and judging whether primary
   actions are visible, whether rows overlap, whether spacing and contrast
   are usable.
2. **Diagnosis of a UI layout defect that a cheaper model has already
   attempted and failed to fix.** Cite the failed attempt (worker ID,
   summary of what was tried, what still fails visually) before pinning Opus.
3. **Cases the operator names explicitly in the same message** (exact model
   name, or an unambiguous "use Opus for this").

### Decision procedure — Opus not permitted (use Grok or AUTO)

Use `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` or AUTO — **never** Opus — for:

- Backend, API, ORM, database migrations, or test-fixture work.
- Documentation, compliance control mapping, runbooks, and binder prose.
- Lab scripting, deployment scripts, PowerShell.
- **Mechanical UI edits:** label and terminology renames, moving items
  between navigation menus, adding a column, wiring an export. These are
  implementation, not visual design, even though they touch UI files.
- Root-cause analysis of non-UI defects.
- Planning, exploration, and codebase reconnaissance.

### Sequencing — cheap implement, expensive accept

The default shape for UI work is:

1. A Grok or AUTO worker makes the edit and **captures the proof artifact**
   (screenshot and/or bounding-box assertion after a full non-`SkipUiBuild`
   deploy).
2. Opus is spent on **one focused review pass** of that artifact.

Prefer one Opus acceptance pass over Opus owning implementation end to end.
Throttling the reviewer must not become an excuse to skip the screenshot or
the bounding-box assertion — the cheaper implementer remains fully
responsible for capturing proof (see
[`essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc`](essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc)).

Resolved policy both rules agree on:

| Work | Model |
| --- | --- |
| Acceptance review of rendered GUI proof; diagnosis after a cheaper UI fix failed; operator-named Opus cases | Opus-class (this override) |
| UI implementation (including mechanical edits), backend, API, ORM, lab, infrastructure, compliance, documentation, planning, exploration | `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` or AUTO |

### Model inheritance is an amplifier

A subagent launched without an explicit `model` inherits its parent's model.
An Opus-pinned parent that spawns children therefore silently spawns Opus
children, multiplying spend without anyone choosing to.

**Required:** any agent running on an expensive model (Opus / Max /
thinking-high) must either (a) do its own work directly with no children, or
(b) pin `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` or omit `model` only when that omission
resolves to AUTO — never leave child model selection implicit when the parent
is expensive. Prefer an **explicit** `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` pin on every
child an expensive parent launches. Never let expensive-model selection
propagate by inheritance.

### Budget-pressure behavior

When the operator signals that expensive-model budget is constrained
(credit burn, "use AUTO", usage percentage, or equivalent), Opus use
**narrows in breadth, not in the ability to fix a known-broken page**.

Under budget pressure, Opus remains **permitted** for:

1. **Acceptance review of a rendered proof artifact** (one focused pass).
2. **Diagnosis of a UI layout defect that a cheaper model has already
   attempted and failed to fix** — this is exactly the "no other model
   works" case. Bound it: **one focused pass** on the specific defect,
   scoped to diagnosis and the fix direction, not a general re-review of
   the page or a rewrite of the feature. Cite the failed cheap attempt.
3. **Cases the operator names explicitly in the same message.**

What budget pressure **removes** is breadth and waste: speculative Opus
review, second opinions, Opus owning implementation end to end, fleet
fan-out, and retry loops on expensive models. It does **not** remove the
ability to unblock a defect that is actually broken after a cheap pass
failed.

As of **2026-07-28** the operator reported **90% Opus usage**, asked for
AUTO wherever possible, and added: **"Do not sacrifice quality
completion."** The throttle eliminates waste; it does not license
incomplete or unverified work.

### Do not sacrifice quality completion (anti-corner-cutting)

Model throttling never justifies:

- Reducing the scope of a slice, dropping acceptance criteria, or leaving
  a feature partly wired in order to finish sooner.
- Skipping or weakening the visual-proof artifact required by
  [`essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc`](essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc).
- Reporting work as done when verification is pending — pending must be
  reported as pending, with the reason and the blocker named.
- Choosing a cheaper model for work it will predictably get wrong, then
  shipping the result unreviewed. If a cheap pass produces something
  unusable, that is the escalation case (item 2 above), not an acceptable
  outcome.

**Ordering:** correctness and completeness first, then cost. Cost
discipline decides *which model* does a piece of work and *how many
passes* it gets — never *whether* the work is finished or verified.

This override does **not**:

1. **Weaken acceptance evidence** in
   [`essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc`](essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc).
   Primary actions visible at 1280x800 and 1024x768, no overlapping rows/cells,
   bounding-box asserts or screenshots the agent read back, a named evidence
   artifact, and a full deploy with **no** `-SkipUiBuild` before any screenshot
   counts as proof — all stand unchanged. The override is **which model** may
   review rendered output; it does not lower the bar the work must clear.
2. **License Opus fan-out or spend escalation anywhere else.** No Opus across
   a fleet of background workers, no Opus retry loops after a usage or credit
   error, no Opus to supervise or unstick a stuck child. Items 1–6 above remain
   binding for all non-override work.

